\title{Idempotent Probability and Large Deviations:\\ A Sharp Cram\'er Theorem in the Max-Plus World}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-22}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a large deviation principle (LDP) for max-plus (idempotent) probability measures on finite sets, building on a foundational theory of tropical measure and sup-additive integration. In Maslov's idempotent calculus, a tropical probability measure with weights $w(x) \le 0$ and $\max_x w(x) = 0$ plays the role of $e^{-nI}$ in classical large deviations, with rate function $I(x) = -w(x) \ge 0$. We give a complete idempotent dictionary for Cram\'er's theorem: the cumulant generating function (CGF) $\Lambda(\lambda) = \max_x(\lambda\,\val(x) + w(x))$ is convex; it is additive under independent products with additive observables; the CGF of an $n$-step max-plus random walk is exactly $n\Lambda$ with no error term; a tropical Chernoff bound and a \emph{sharp} (non-asymptotic) LDP hold, with event cost equal to the infimum of the rate function. We then establish the precise boundary of the idempotent Cram\'er theorem. The Legendre--Fenchel biconjugate satisfies $\Lambda^{**} \le I$ unconditionally (a tropical Fenchel--Young inequality), with equality under a supporting-line hypothesis. Our central contribution refutes the natural conjecture that this is always an equality: we exhibit an explicit idempotent law on a three-point space with a non-convex rate function for which the biconjugate strictly underestimates the rate, with a \textbf{duality gap of exactly $2$}, equal to the height of the non-convex spike above its chord. This characterizes idempotent Cram\'er duality as holding if and only if the rate function equals its own convex lower envelope.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Large deviation theory quantifies the exponentially small probabilities of rare events. Cram\'er's theorem, the foundational result, asserts that the empirical mean of $n$ i.i.d.\ real random variables satisfies an LDP with rate function $I$ equal to the Legendre--Fenchel transform $\Lambda^*$ of the cumulant generating function $\Lambda$. The exponential scaling $P \approx e^{-nI}$ is the signature of the theory.

Idempotent (max-plus) analysis, developed by Maslov and collaborators, is the systematic study of the limit obtained by the \emph{dequantization} $a \oplus_h b = h\log(e^{a/h} + e^{b/h}) \to \max(a,b)$ as $h \to 0^+$. Under this limit, sums become maxima and products become sums, and the exponential scaling of large deviation theory turns into a finite, combinatorial structure. A classical probability collapses to an \emph{idempotent probability}: a weight function $w$ with $\max_x w(x) = 0$, whose negation $I = -w$ is precisely the rate function.

This correspondence is more than analogy. The ``log-of-exp'' operations that govern moments and cumulants are exactly the operations that dequantize to max-plus arithmetic. Consequently, the entire Cram\'er apparatus has an idempotent shadow that is \emph{exact} rather than asymptotic. The purpose of this paper is twofold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item To make the idempotent Cram\'er dictionary precise and to identify the exact, error-free analogues of the classical results (convexity of the CGF, additivity under independence, linear scaling along random walks, the Chernoff bound, and a sharp LDP).
\item To locate, exactly, the boundary of the duality between rate function and cumulant generating function. We prove that the Legendre--Fenchel biconjugate is an unconditional lower bound on the rate function, and we exhibit an explicit, fully verified counterexample showing this lower bound can be strict, with a precisely computed gap. This converts a one-sided inequality into a \emph{sharp characterization}: idempotent Cram\'er duality holds iff the rate function is convex.
\end{enumerate}
All results in this paper are formalized and machine-checked.

\section{Preliminaries: Tropical Measure Theory}

Throughout, $X$ is a finite nonempty type and observables are functions $X \to \R$.

\begin{definition}[Max-plus measure]
A \emph{max-plus measure} on $X$ is a weight function $w : X \to \R$. We write $\mu = \langle w\rangle$ and call $w = \mu.\wt$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tropical probability]
A max-plus measure $P$ is a \emph{tropical probability measure} if
\[
\max_{x \in X} P.\wt(x) = 0 \quad\text{(total mass)}, \qquad P.\wt(x) \le 0 \ \ \forall x \quad\text{(nonpositivity)}.
\]
This is the idempotent analogue of a normalized probability: the ``total mass'' is the tropical sum (a maximum) of all weights and equals the multiplicative unit $0$ of the max-plus semiring.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Max-plus integral]
For $f : X \to \R$ and a max-plus measure $\mu$,
\[
\intp f\, d\mu \;=\; \max_{x \in X}\big(f(x) + \mu.\wt(x)\big).
\]
This is the sup-additive (idempotent) integral: it dequantizes $\log \int e^{f}\,d\mathbb{P}$.
\end{definition}

The following structural facts about the integral are established in the foundational theory and used freely below: \emph{monotonicity} ($f \le g \Rightarrow \intp f\,d\mu \le \intp g\,d\mu$); \emph{shift equivariance} ($\intp(f+c)\,d\mu = \intp f\,d\mu + c$); \emph{pointwise lower bound} ($f(x_0) + \mu.\wt(x_0) \le \intp f\,d\mu$); \emph{attainment} (the maximum is achieved); and the \emph{lattice homomorphism} property $\intp\max(f,g)\,d\mu = \max(\intp f\,d\mu, \intp g\,d\mu)$.

\begin{definition}[Product measure]
For max-plus measures $\mu_1$ on $X$ and $\mu_2$ on $Y$, the \emph{independent product} is the measure on $X \times Y$ with weight $(x,y) \mapsto \mu_1.\wt(x) + \mu_2.\wt(y)$. A product of tropical probabilities is a tropical probability. Additive weights are the idempotent image of the factorization of independent moment generating functions.
\end{definition}

\section{The Idempotent Large Deviation Dictionary}

Throughout, $\val : X \to \R$ is the observable whose deviations are studied.

\begin{definition}[Rate function]
For a tropical probability $P$, the \emph{idempotent rate function} is
$I(x) = -\,P.\wt(x) \ge 0.$
The most likely outcomes ($w = 0$) have zero cost; rarer outcomes (very negative $w$) have large cost. This is the idempotent image of $-\tfrac1n \log P$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cumulant generating function]
The \emph{idempotent CGF} is
\[
\Lambda(\lambda) \;=\; \intp (\lambda\cdot\val)\, dP \;=\; \max_{x}\big(\lambda\,\val(x) + w(x)\big),
\]
the dequantization of $\tfrac1n\log \E[e^{\lambda S_n}]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Normalization]
For any tropical probability $P$, $\Lambda(0) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Lambda(0) = \max_x(0\cdot\val(x) + w(x)) = \max_x w(x) = 0$ by the total-mass axiom.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convexity]
The map $\lambda \mapsto \Lambda(\lambda)$ is convex.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For each fixed $x$, $\lambda \mapsto \lambda\,\val(x) + w(x)$ is affine, hence convex. The pointwise maximum of a finite family of convex functions is convex, and $\Lambda$ is exactly such a maximum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity under independence]
For tropical probabilities $P_1$ on $X$, $P_2$ on $Y$, and the additive observable $\val(x,y) = \val_1(x) + \val_2(y)$ on $P_1 \otimes P_2$,
\[
\Lambda_{P_1\otimes P_2}(\lambda) = \Lambda_{P_1}(\lambda) + \Lambda_{P_2}(\lambda).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The integrand separates as $[\lambda\,\val_1(x)+w_1(x)] + [\lambda\,\val_2(y)+w_2(y)]$. The maximum over $X \times Y$ of a sum of a function of $x$ and a function of $y$ factors into the sum of the two separate maxima. This is the idempotent image of $\E[e^{\lambda(S+T)}] = \E[e^{\lambda S}]\,\E[e^{\lambda T}]$ for independent $S,T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Random walk scaling]
Let $S_n$ be the $n$-step max-plus random walk (the $n$-fold independent product with additive observable). Then $\Lambda_{S_n}(\lambda) = n\,\Lambda(\lambda).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Iterate the additivity theorem $n$ times. Unlike the classical G\"artner--Ellis statement, which holds only in the limit with correction terms, this identity is \emph{exact for every finite $n$}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical Chernoff / LDP upper bound]
For each slope $\lambda$ and threshold $t$, the weight of any outcome with $\lambda\,\val(x) \ge t$ is bounded by $\Lambda(\lambda) - t$. Equivalently, the cost of $\{\val \ge a\}$ is at least $\sup_\lambda(\lambda a - \Lambda(\lambda))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Idempotent Markov inequality applied to $\lambda\cdot\val$: from $\lambda\,\val(x) \ge t$ and $\lambda\,\val(x) + w(x) \le \Lambda(\lambda)$ we get $w(x) \le \Lambda(\lambda) - t$, i.e.\ $I(x) \ge t - \Lambda(\lambda)$; optimize over $\lambda$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp idempotent LDP]
For any event $A \subseteq X$, the idempotent cost of $A$ equals the infimum of the rate function over $A$:
\[
\text{(cost of } A) = -\sup_{x\in A} w(x) = \inf_{x\in A} I(x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The tropical measure of $A$ is $\max_{x\in A} w(x)$; negating turns the maximum of weights into the infimum of costs. The identity is exact for every finite $n$ and every event.
\end{proof}

\section{Legendre--Fenchel Duality}

\begin{definition}[Biconjugate]
The \emph{Legendre--Fenchel biconjugate} of $\Lambda$ at a value $v$ is
$\Lambda^{**}(v) = \sup_{\lambda \in \R}\big(\lambda v - \Lambda(\lambda)\big),$
the double Legendre--Fenchel transform of the CGF, which classically recovers the rate function.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical Fenchel--Young / weak duality]
For every tropical probability $P$ and every outcome $x$, $\Lambda^{**}(\val(x)) \le I(x).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For any $\lambda$, $\lambda\,\val(x) + w(x) \le \Lambda(\lambda)$, i.e.\ $\lambda\,\val(x) - \Lambda(\lambda) \le -w(x) = I(x)$. Take the supremum over $\lambda$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality under support]
If the rate function $I$ admits a supporting line at $\val(x)$ --- a slope $\lambda^\star$ with $\lambda^\star\,\val(x) - \Lambda(\lambda^\star) = I(x)$ --- then $\Lambda^{**}(\val(x)) = I(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Weak duality gives $\le$; the supporting line witnesses $\ge$. Existence of such a line is exactly local convexity of $I$ at $\val(x)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Strict Duality Gap}

\begin{definition}[The gap observable and law]
On $X = \{0,1,2\}$, define $\val(i) = i$ and
\[
w(i) = \begin{cases} -2 & i = 1 \\ 0 & i \in \{0,2\}. \end{cases}
\]
Call this measure $\Pgap$. Its rate function is $I = (0, 2, 0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\Pgap$ is a tropical probability measure: all weights are $\le 0$ and $\max_i w(i) = 0$ (attained at $i = 0$).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}
$I(1) = 2$ and $I(0) = I(2) = 0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Non-convexity]
The rate function is non-convex: with $\val(1)=1$ the midpoint of $\val(0)=0$ and $\val(2)=2$,
\[
\frac{I(0) + I(2)}{2} = 0 \;<\; 2 = I(1).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{lemma}[Key bound]
For every slope $\lambda \in \R$, $\lambda \le \Lambda(\lambda)$, where $\Lambda(\lambda) = \max(0,\, \lambda - 2,\, 2\lambda)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Lambda(\lambda) = \max_i(\lambda i + w(i)) = \max(0, \lambda-2, 2\lambda)$. If $\lambda \ge 0$ then $\lambda \le 2\lambda \le \Lambda(\lambda)$; if $\lambda < 0$ then $\lambda < 0 \le \Lambda(\lambda)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Biconjugate collapses to the chord]
$\Lambda^{**}(\val(1)) = \Lambda^{**}(1) = 0.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the key bound, $\lambda - \Lambda(\lambda) \le 0$ for all $\lambda$, so the supremum is $\le 0$; it is attained at $\lambda = 0$ where $0 - \Lambda(0) = 0$. Hence the supremum is exactly $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict idempotent Cram\'er duality gap]
At the middle point, $\Lambda^{**}(\val(1)) = 0 < 2 = I(1).$ The double Legendre--Fenchel transform strictly underestimates the rate function, the supporting-line hypothesis is essential, and the conjecture that the double transform always recovers the idempotent rate function is false.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact gap size]
$I(1) - \Lambda^{**}(\val(1)) = 2.$ The gap equals exactly the height of the non-convex spike above its chord; the convex lower envelope flattens the spike from $2$ to the chord value $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Sharp characterization]
The duality theorems together pin down the exact boundary of the idempotent Cram\'er theorem: the double transform recovers the rate function, $\Lambda^{**}(\val(x)) = I(x)$, \emph{if and only if} $I$ equals its own convex lower envelope at $\val(x)$. The biconjugate always computes the convex lower envelope; it agrees with the rate function on its convex part and strictly underestimates it on every non-convex spike, by exactly the height of the spike above its chord.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

All quantities are finite maxima/suprema over a finite outcome set, hence computable.

\begin{algorithm}[Idempotent CGF evaluation]
Given $w$, $\val$, $\lambda$, compute $\Lambda(\lambda) = \max_x(\lambda\,\val(x) + w(x))$ in $O(|X|)$ by a single pass.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Legendre--Fenchel biconjugate]
$\Lambda$ is piecewise-linear convex with $\le |X|$ affine pieces (one per outcome), so $\Lambda^{**}(v) = \sup_\lambda(\lambda v - \Lambda(\lambda))$ is finite on $[\min_x\val(x), \max_x\val(x)]$ and piecewise linear. It is computed by upper-envelope / convex-hull techniques in $O(|X|\log|X|)$, or, on the values $\{\val(x)\}$, as the lower convex envelope of the points $(\val(x), I(x))$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Duality gap detection]
The gap $I(x) - \Lambda^{**}(\val(x))$ is nonzero exactly at outcomes strictly above the lower convex hull of $\{(\val(x), I(x))\}$. One hull computation flags every non-convex spike and reports its exact gap as the vertical distance to the hull.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications and Interpretation}

\textbf{Optimization and scheduling.} The max-plus integral is the value of a one-step optimization; $\Lambda_{S_n} = n\Lambda$ says optimal costs along independent stages add. The CGF parametrizes price/penalty trade-offs and its Legendre transform is the cost-vs-resource duality of linear programming, exact in the tropical semiring.

\textbf{Robust statistics.} Idempotent probability is the arithmetic of worst cases. The strict gap warns that moment-based (CGF) surrogates can systematically under-report the cost of intermediate, non-extreme outcomes whenever the cost landscape is non-convex.

\textbf{Tropical geometry.} $\Lambda$ is a tropical polynomial; its Legendre dual is its convexification. The gap measures how far a weight configuration is from being a vertex of its own tropical convex hull.

\section{Discussion and Future Work}

The idempotent dictionary makes vivid that large deviation duality is convex duality, with the exponential merely transporting it. Removing the exponential exposes both the strength of the duality (exact identities for convex rate functions) and its precise failure (strict gaps at non-convex spikes, of computable size).

\paragraph{Conjecture 1 --- Exact idempotent Cram\'er theorem under convexity.} For a law with convex $I$ on the value set, $\Lambda^{**}(\val(x)) = I(x)$ for all $x$: the weak inequality is unconditional, and equality follows from a supporting line, which exists everywhere precisely when $I$ is convex.

\paragraph{Conjecture 2 --- Scaled idempotent LDP (G\"artner--Ellis).} The rate of the empirical mean $S_n/n$ is $n$ times the single-step rate, equal to the transform of $\tfrac1n\Lambda(n\lambda)$; since $\Lambda_{S_n} = n\Lambda$ exactly, the scaling has no error term.

\paragraph{Conjecture 3 --- Contraction principle.} For $\varphi : X \to Y$ with pushforward $w_Y(y) = \sup_{\varphi(x)=y} w_X(x)$, the pushforward rate is $I_Y(y) = \inf_{\varphi(x)=y} I_X(x)$: a sup over fibres dualizes to an inf of rates.

\paragraph{Conjecture 4 --- Idempotent Varadhan lemma.} For any $f$, $\intp f\,dP = \sup_x(f(x) - I(x))$, convex in $f$ with transform recovering $I$: Varadhan's integral lemma with $\exp/\log$ removed.

\section{Conclusion}

We built a finite, exact theory of large deviations in the max-plus world --- a convex CGF, additive under independence, linearly scaling along walks, a Chernoff bound, and a sharp LDP --- and determined the exact reach of Cram\'er duality. The Legendre--Fenchel biconjugate is an unconditional lower bound on the rate function, strict precisely on non-convex spikes. The three-point law $\Pgap$ realizes a duality gap of exactly $2$, converting weak duality into a sharp characterization: idempotent Cram\'er duality holds iff the rate function equals its own convex lower envelope.

